SET-IDENTITY AGAINST PART I, AND WHY THE COMPARISON IS SORTED RATHER THAN
BYTE-ORDERED. This exporter does NOT render in a stable order: the same test on
the same binary emits the same 33 families in a different sequence run to run,
because the render walks a hash map. A raw `diff` of the two captures is
therefore all noise and no signal, and anyone who runs one will see ~90 changed
lines that mean nothing. The predicate this gate actually asserts is over the
SET -- every pinned name present, every value 0 -- so the comparison is made
over the sorted lines:

  $ grep -E '^(# TYPE )?topgun_or_prune_' <part I render>   | sort > set-old
  $ grep -E '^(# TYPE )?topgun_or_prune_' <re-captured render> | sort > set-head
  $ diff set-old set-head
  (no output)                              <- 122 lines each, IDENTICAL

  So the repair moved neither the registered name set nor any first-scrape
  value. The full byte listing in Part I is thereby re-proven current; it is not
  reprinted here, because reprinting 160 lines of zeros would invite exactly the
  "which run produced this?" ambiguity this Part exists to remove.

  LIMB (a): PASS at the final instrument.

